# Nonequilibrium and Information
#
# From the demon Maxwell imagined in 1867 to the single-bit erasures measured in 2012, and the fluctuation theorems that let small systems break the second law for a moment.

#>physics_nonequilibrium
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!physics
#:form #!timeline
#:level #!graduate
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bonk
#:era #!modern

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Nonequilibrium and Information"
 ~#claim: "Erasing a bit costs at least kT ln 2 of heat, about 2.9e-21 J at 300 K; on the Landauer-Bennett account, that cost is what saves the second law from Maxwell's demon."
 ~#proof: "Landauer 1961 and Bennett 1982 for the argument, Norton 2005 for the dispute over it; Berut and colleagues 2012 and Jun and colleagues 2014 for the measurements."
 ~#door: "Walk the ladder from the demon to the colloid bead, then ask what a living cell pays per error."
 ~#audience: "graduate students and curious engineers who have met entropy and want its price in bits"
 continuity: "Nonequilibrium and Information | erasing a bit"
 cold_open: "Erasing a bit always warms the room a little, and on one leading account that tiny heat keeps Maxwell's demon honest."
}

